---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Callout from "../components/Callout.astro";

// Visual walkthrough of the operating system. Screenshots live in /public.
// Drop the four sanitized PNGs in with these exact names to populate the figures:
//   os-01-architecture.png  ·  os-02-memory.png  ·  os-03-agents.png  ·  os-04-integrations.png
const exhibits = [
  {
    id: "architecture",
    num: "01",
    img: "/os-01-architecture.png",
    label: "Context architecture",
    title: "One system, many projects",
    caption:
      "Every project carries a CLAUDE.md that auto-loads who I am, the project's state, and the rules I work by. A session starts already knowing where things stand instead of starting cold. The structure and the rules are mine; the model reads them.",
  },
  {
    id: "memory",
    num: "02",
    img: "/os-02-memory.png",
    label: "Memory between sessions",
    title: "Status files as the source of truth",
    caption:
      "At the end of a session I run a /session-end skill that drafts where we are, what got decided, what is open, and what is next. I review and close it. The next morning picks up exactly there. What makes it work is keeping the ritual, not the way the model writes it up.",
  },
  {
    id: "agents",
    num: "03",
    img: "/os-03-agents.png",
    label: "Scoped agents, with kill criteria",
    title: "I build agents like products",
    caption:
      "Each agent has a defined scope, its own context, and decision authority. The part I care about most is the activation and retirement criteria: this one is dormant, with an explicit rule for when it comes back. I retire agents when the project pivots, the same way I would kill a feature that is not earning its place.",
  },
  {
    id: "integrations",
    num: "04",
    img: "/os-04-integrations.png",
    label: "Integrations and automation",
    title: "Reaching into the tools the work lives in",
    caption:
      "MCP connectors give the system read access to Slack, Asana, and meeting transcripts, and a one-way path to Drive for sharing out. A few scheduled automations run on their own, like drafting a weekly agenda and posting it, with a check so it never double-posts. The rules (no automated deletes, manual approval on anything touching shared systems) came from iteration, including one bad day with a sync script.",
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  sideBarActiveItemID="operating-system"
  title="The Operating System | Agueda Schwartz"
  description="A sanitized look at the operating system I built on Claude Code to run a 0-to-1 AI portfolio: context architecture, session memory, scoped agents, and live integrations."
  maxWidthClass="lg:max-w-[1000px]"
>
  <div class="mt-5">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold leading-tight mb-2">The operating system</h1>
    <div class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wider opacity-50 mb-6 lg:pb-4 lg:border-b lg:border-base-300">
      What it actually looks like
    </div>

    <div class="prose max-w-none">
      <p>
        I ran a 0-to-1 AI portfolio end to end. To do that, I built an
        operating system on Claude Code that holds the context, the memory, and the routine work,
        so I can spend my judgment on what is worth building and what to kill. This page is the
        system itself, sanitized. The longer write-up of what it is teaching me lives in
        <a href="/practice">Working with Claude Code as a Product Manager</a>.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm opacity-70">
        Screens are redacted: no participant names, no partner details, no proprietary work. The
        point is the structure, not the contents.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-10 space-y-14">
      {exhibits.map((ex) => (
        <section id={ex.id} class="not-prose">
          <div class="flex items-baseline gap-3 mb-3">
            <span class="font-mono text-xs opacity-50 font-semibold">{ex.num}</span>
            <span class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.15em] opacity-50 font-semibold">{ex.label}</span>
          </div>
          <h2 class="text-xl font-bold mb-4 mt-0">{ex.title}</h2>
          <figure class="m-0">
            <div class="rounded-lg border border-base-300 bg-base-200 overflow-hidden">
              <img
                src={ex.img}
                alt={`Screenshot: ${ex.title}`}
                loading="lazy"
                class="block w-full h-auto"
              />
            </div>
            <figcaption class="text-base leading-relaxed mt-4 max-w-2xl">
              {ex.caption}
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
        </section>
      ))}
    </div>

    <div class="mt-14">
      <Callout title="Where the line sits">
        <p>
          The structure, the success criteria, and the calls about what to build and what to kill
          are mine. The model builds and wires. I am not writing production software; I am
          engineering the leverage around the work. That line is the whole point: it is how I would
          help an organization adopt AI, design the system, define what good looks like, measure it,
          and retire what does not work.
        </p>
      </Callout>
    </div>

    <div class="prose max-w-none mt-12">
      <h2>What this means for a team adopting AI</h2>
      <p>
        Most AI efforts stall on adoption, not on the technology. I built this system for myself
        first, but the method is the part that travels: design the system, decide up front what good
        looks like, measure whether people actually use it, and retire what does not earn its place.
        That is the discipline I would bring to helping an organization or its customers adopt AI,
        the same way I built it here, one decision at a time.
      </p>
      <p>
        For the longer story, the pivot it surfaced, the pain points it solved, and what I am still
        figuring out, read <a href="/practice">the full write-up →</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  h2[id], section[id] {
    scroll-margin-top: 2rem;
  }
</style>
